-- Reading: timed reading of dense texts.

A single teaspoon of healthy soil may contain more organisms than there are people on Earth. Most are bacteria and fungi, invisible to the eye, yet they determine whether plants thrive. Fungi form threads that connect the roots of neighbouring plants, carrying water and nutrients between them; some ecologists describe these networks, with deliberate exaggeration, as an underground internet. Intensive ploughing breaks the threads, and heavy use of fertiliser can make plants less dependent on fungi, so the networks shrink. Farmers who reduce ploughing often see yields dip for two or three seasons before soil life recovers, which explains why many are reluctant to change.', 'VALID');
