A. Cities are often several degrees warmer than the countryside around them, an effect known as the urban heat island. Dark roofs and roads absorb sunlight during the day and release the heat at night, so the city never cools down.

B. Planting trees is the best-known remedy, but trees take decades to mature and need water that dry cities may not have. Painting roofs white reflects sunlight immediately and costs little, although the effect is smaller in winter, when warmth is welcome.

C. Planners increasingly combine measures: reflective surfaces for quick results, trees for the long term, and fountains or shaded walkways where people gather. No single solution, they argue, works everywhere.', 'VALID');
