-- Course test: Reading and Listening.

Apps that let people rent out spare rooms, cars or tools were first hailed as a way to use resources more efficiently. In practice, the outcome has depended on regulation. In some cities, flats were withdrawn from the rental market and let to tourists all year, which pushed up rents for residents. Elsewhere, limits on the number of nights a home can be let have kept the original idea alive: residents earn extra income without the housing supply shrinking. The lesson, it seems, is not that sharing apps are harmful but that their effects are shaped by the rules around them.', 'VALID');
